    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Extensive bleaching of the Jurassic eolian Navajo- Nugget-Aztec sandstone complex on and around the Colorado Plateau has been interpreted as evidence of hydrocarbon-related fluid flow on a massive scale during Late Cretaceous to Early Tertiary time. Close association of profound bleaching with structural and stratigraphic traps within these now exhumed sandstones suggests a cumulative paleo-reservoir potential far surpassing that of the Ghawar oil field in Saudi Arabia. Expansive outcrops of bleached Aztec Sandstone in the Valley of Fire, NV afford an excellent opportunity to assess how one such paleo-reservoir possibly containing more than 1x109 barrels oil would have performed if it had been developed at that time.\r\n\r\nAbundant geologic evidence indicates that pervasive arrays of relatively low porosity/permeability compaction bands (CBs) comprised the dominant structural fabric then present in an otherwise high- porosity sandstone typified by large amplitude eolian cross bedding. Using a high-resolution aerial photographic base, we are compiling a map detailing patterns of individual, cm-thick CBs over an outcrop area covering tens of acres (>100,000 m2). Two- dimensional flow simulations performed on this unprecedented data set allow us to investigate how such a CB-rich paleo-reservoir—and by extension modern sandstone reservoirs like it—could most efficiently have been produced.\r\n\r\nPreliminary results, obtained for a 7-acre outcrop area and using a discrete fracture modeling method that honors each individual CB, reveal a potentially profound influence on flow and communication between a producer/injector well pair spaced more than 240 m apart. For realistic values of CB thickness and permeability, the pressure required to drive flow across the dominant band pattern is about 3 times greater than would be expected in the absence of CBs, and about 50% more than needed to drive flow along the pattern. These large-scale flow effects could reasonably be approximated using a homogeneous permeability anisotropy with kmax oriented parallel to the dominant CB trend. Thus, armed with only spatially limited data on the orientation, volume fraction and relative permeability of CBs then present, a paleo-reservoir engineer could have designed a well pattern aligned with the dominant CB grain to most efficiently produce the paleo Aztec reservoir.",
